{
  "packet_id": "38528ff3a59052d7",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether to invest, and what evidence would justify it) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including maintaining the status quo and licensing the brand or technology. It also correctly marks the €20,000 budget and 12-week timeline as analyst assumptions. It does not correct a premise or surface a non-obvious constraint. Listing 'construction' as a consumer use case blurs the consumer and workplace boundary rather than sharpening it."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9), the best theory, states who changes behaviour (consumers who value style) and why (lifestyle framing raises willingness to buy). It also names how value is captured: premium price, brand extension and licensing. The link from behaviour change to value captured is asserted rather than traced. For example, 'purchase multiple pairs' is stated without support, and nothing explains why this manufacturer, rather than lifestyle brands, would capture the premium."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (lifestyle accessory) and T2 (activity-specific safety need) are genuine rival explanations of what drives consumer adoption. They gesture at different channels: fashion and lifestyle channels versus safety partnerships. However, both are operationalised only as message framings (A19), not as clearly different products or customer groups. T2 also blends consumers with employers and workplace regulation."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable: purchase intent and willingness to pay compared with a standard or general product. Some are vague, such as 'markets where fashion and lifestyle are valued'. Some assumptions, for example 'Safety regulations ... require hearing protection for specific activities', are not tied to consumer purchase. It is not made clear which failed prediction would sink each theory."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A16", "A18"],
      "reason": "A18 classifies apple-en352, which is a company claim, as an 'independent_study'. It then marks it as supporting T1 while its own limits note says the source 'does not address consumer perception of hearing protection as a lifestyle accessory'. A12 marks hlb-head, a head-protection positioning goal, as supporting T2, and A16 does the same with the hlb-junior product description. In both cases the limits note says the source does not show the claimed perception. The 'supports' labels therefore contradict the stated limits. A11 handles bogoch-2005-appearance reasonably, but no assessment discusses date (2005), place, or whether the free-earplugs-at-the-door setting fits paying consumers."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "A13 runs one search against T1, but the sources it found (hearingtracker-2026-loop-price, iso-founded, uvx-consumer) are never assessed or reported. The trace does not say whether contrary evidence was found. No search is made against T2. The theory was not genuinely tested against what the search surfaced."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A19"],
      "reason": "A19 randomises lifestyle versus safety messaging and gives opposing predictions for T1 and T2, with a three-way decision rule. This separates the two theories. The €15,000 cost, 8-week timeline, sample of 500, and the 15-point and €20 thresholds are unjustified assumptions. Stated intent in a survey is weak evidence of real purchase. The design also does not test whether Hellberg can capture value, as opposed to the category as a whole."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A21"],
      "reason": "The scheduled update SYN-C was delivered and noted, but the response is unsound. A21 treats a result explicitly labelled 'SIMULATED RESULT, NOT REAL DATA' as support for T1. It also stretches a wear-frequency finding into a new claim that styling 'increases wear frequency and purchase intent'. Purchase intent was never measured, even in the simulation."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A20", "A22"],
      "reason": "A22 records that the investigation stopped before a recommendation because of repeated errors. A20 is an interim position, not a completed recommendation. Under the protocol this stage is not reached and contributes zero to the all-case composite."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "incomplete",
      "event_ids": ["A22"],
      "reason": "The episode ended with 'stopped before a recommendation (repeated errors)', so there is no completed recommendation."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A21"],
      "reason": "A simulated SYN-C result is treated as real support for T1. The revised claim also adds 'purchase intent', which SYN-C does not report (it covers wear frequency only)."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "apple-en352, a company claim about a media-volume limit feature, is mislabelled 'independent_study'. It is marked as supporting T1 despite saying nothing about lifestyle perception, as A18's own limits note admits."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A16"],
      "reason": "Company marketing statements (a head-protection positioning goal, and a description of the Junior product line) are marked as supporting T2's demand claim. The agent's own limits notes say they do not show that consumers perceive hearing protection as an activity requirement. This treats marketing claims as evidence of demand."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A20"],
      "reason": "Rating T1 'likely' and proposing investment rests on one indirect 2005 correlational study (A11) and a mislabelled company claim (A18). The evidence does not support that level of belief."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "The challenge search results, including hearingtracker-2026-loop-price, were never assessed or reported, so the challenge outcome is undisclosed."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "Rejected. The episode is incomplete, with no recommendation (A22). It also contains an unsound update that treats a simulated result as real and extends it to purchase intent (A21), plus multiple unsupported 'supports' assessments, including misclassifying a company claim as an independent study (A18) and treating marketing claims as demand evidence (A12, A16). Any one of these is a critical error."
}

{
  "packet_id": "737fb2b82acc1e08",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including no expansion, a route-to-market change and redesign. It finds no non-obvious option and corrects no premise. The example use cases 'construction, medical' blur consumer and B2B segments. The €20,000 / 12-week budget is an assumption, not given in the case."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A9), is a circular conditional: consumers adopt 'if they perceive hearing protection as a lifestyle accessory'. The claim that consumers will treat earplugs as 'a fashion item and a status symbol' and 'purchase multiple pairs' is asserted, not explained. A9 names a benefit and premium pricing, but gives no grounded account of why behaviour changes or how Hellberg in particular captures value."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (lifestyle) and T2 (medical necessity) are genuine rival explanations and nominally imply different channels (specialty/DTC vs medical retailers). In practice both are messaging frames for the same product. They share an identical repeat-purchase prediction, and neither is developed into a distinct product or customer offering."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "Predictions are observable, for example 'willingness to pay at least 20% more' (A9) and '30% more' (A10). The thresholds are arbitrary assumptions. The repeat-purchase prediction is identical across T1 and T2, so it cannot embarrass either theory specifically. The assumptions are stated but largely restate the theory."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A13"],
      "reason": "Sources are labelled company_assertion or independent_study, and each assessment has a 'Does not show' note (A11, A12, A14). However, bogoch-2005-appearance links lack of concern about earplug appearance with willingness to use free protection. That bears directly on T1's design premise, yet A14 assesses it only against T2. hlb-head (A11) is about head protection, not hearing. crutzen-2021 and apple-en352 were found but never assessed. Date, place and population fit are never addressed."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A16"],
      "reason": "A13 searches against T1 and surfaces crutzen-2021-celebrate-safe and sdn-shop. Neither result is assessed or reported, and the position in A16 proceeds as if nothing was found. The search is made but not reported fairly."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A15"],
      "reason": "The randomized lifestyle-vs-medical messaging WTP survey separates T1 from T2 in relative terms. Several weaknesses keep it below rung 3:\n- The general 18–55 panel does not target T2's 'hearing sensitivity' population.\n- It compares the frames with each other rather than with a standard earplug, as the theories' own predictions require.\n- It does not test the no-expansion alternative.\n- The decision rule is truncated ('no.') and leaves a gap between thresholds.\n- Stated WTP only.\n- The €15,000 / 6-week cost is an assumption."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A17"],
      "reason": "A17 correctly treats SYN-D as simulated and irrelevant (warehouse label printer, no wearers or buyers measured) and retains T1. This is a proportionate, evidence-linked retention. The implications for the recommendation are not explicitly traced."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A16", "A18", "A11", "A12", "A14"],
      "reason": "A18 recommends 'Invest in a differentiated consumer proposition targeting lifestyle demand' and rates T1 'likely'. Every assessment was neutral (A11, A12, A14), so the position is unsupported. It does name a next action (the survey) and reversal conditions. But it commits to investment ahead of the validation it proposes, and the target customers listed are not derived from any evidence."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A16", "A18", "A11", "A12", "A14"],
      "reason": "T1 is rated 'likely' and investment is recommended, although all assessed evidence was neutral and no source supports lifestyle demand or a premium."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "The claim that consumers will view earplugs as 'a fashion item and a status symbol' and 'purchase multiple pairs' is presented as the mechanism without any supporting source."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A16"],
      "reason": "The challenge search surfaced crutzen-2021-celebrate-safe and sdn-shop, but neither was assessed or reported. Challenge results were effectively ignored."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "bogoch-2005-appearance is relevant to T1's design/appearance premise but was assessed only against T2 as neutral, which misdirects its relevance."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A15"],
      "reason": "The test's decision rule is truncated and incomplete. The general consumer sample does not reach T2's hearing-sensitive population, which weakens discrimination."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode completes and handles the simulated update correctly (A17). It is rejected because the final recommendation (A16/A18) rests on unsupported inference: T1 is rated 'likely' and investment recommended with no supporting evidence. In addition, challenge results were ignored (A13) and a relevant study was misdirected (A14)."
}

{
  "packet_id": "abbb75e13320643a",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 lists the obvious alternatives: home consumer device, B2B expansion, status quo, a different segment and retail partnership. It does not clearly separate the underlying objective (profitable growth from BIA technology) from the suggested solution, and it corrects no premise. The euro-denominated €20,000/12-week budget is anachronistic for 1993 and is only an assumption. A1 also restricts evidence to pre-1993 public sources, but later events break that restriction (A12)."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A9), names a beneficiary and a generic motive ('value evidence-based health decisions'). It does not explain why body-fat readings would change purchase behaviour beyond a weight scale. Value capture is asserted as 'premium price' and 'reputation for medical accuracy' without a chain. T2 (A10) relies on an 'existing distribution network' that no source establishes. This amounts to naming a benefit, not tracing a mechanism."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (accuracy motive) and T2 (convenience motive) share the same product, customer pool, retail channel and premium pricing. Their predictions are near-identical ('purchase intent will be higher than for a generic bathroom scale'). They are largely rewordings of one consumer-adoption idea, not rivals implying different products, customers or channels."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "Assumptions and predictions are stated. However, the main prediction in A9 and A10 is the same for both theories, so its failure would not distinguish or seriously endanger either one. 'Perceived health benefit' and 'perceived convenience' are vague, and no thresholds or observable discriminators are given."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15", "A16", "A17"],
      "reason": "A11 correctly marks tanita-dic-origin as a company assertion that does not show demand. Other assessments misread sources. A12 relabels a verified observation as an 'independent_study' and uses a present-day statement ('most nowadays keep track of your body composition') as support for a 1993 convenience theory, without noting its date or fit. A14 treats Radley's finding on overweight and obese children as contradicting the convenience theory, although it concerns accuracy in one subgroup. A15 treats Jebb's modest mean bias (0.8 kg) as a contradiction of convenience, which it does not address. A16 and A17 paste the same 'does not show' text for both theories, even when it is irrelevant to T1. A16 and A17 also omit Lukaski's caveat that further validation is needed in subjects with abnormal body composition. No assessment states date or place limits."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14", "A15"],
      "reason": "A13 searches against T1, but the results are then assigned as contradictions to T2 (A14, A15), so T1 is never actually challenged. The 'contradictions' to T2 are forced: neither Radley nor Jebb bears on convenience-driven demand. The investigation looked but did not report fairly what the search found."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "The episode stopped at A18 ('repeated errors') before any test was proposed or declined."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": [],
      "reason": "Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions, and no update was scheduled or delivered."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "A18 records that the investigation stopped before a recommendation."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "incomplete",
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "The episode stopped due to repeated errors, with no test design and no recommendation."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A14", "A15"],
      "reason": "Accuracy findings (Radley on overweight children; Jebb on mean bias) are asserted as contradicting a convenience-demand theory they do not address. These contradictions appear manufactured for balance."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "A present-day household observation ('nowadays') is used as support for a 1993 consumer-convenience theory. This relies on later outcomes and breaks A1's own pre-1993 constraint."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A10"],
      "reason": "T2's value capture depends on an 'existing distribution network' to consumers, which no source supports."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A16", "A17"],
      "reason": "The Lukaski quote omits the source's own caveat about validation in abnormal body composition. The 'does not show' limits are copy-pasted across theories without relevance to T1."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A3", "A4", "A5", "A6", "A7", "A8"],
      "reason": "Searches report finding 'tanita-en-share', which is not in the source dossier. This is a phantom or unverifiable source reference."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The predicted observation for both theories is identical (purchase intent above a generic scale), so it cannot separate the rivals."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "Rejected. The episode is incomplete: it stopped at A18 with no experiment and no recommendation. It also contains several unsupported inferences, including forced contradictions (A14, A15), use of a present-day observation as 1993 support (A12) and an unsupported distribution claim (A10). Its theories are non-discriminating (A9, A10)."
}

{
  "packet_id": "4c93df17e7f5d4dc",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether to invest, and what evidence would justify it) from the suggested solution. It lists three real alternatives, including selling existing coatings without differentiation and not expanding. It does not catch any non-obvious constraint or false premise. Its examples ('residential marine protection', automotive) are vague, and it never asks whether marine and protective technology has any consumer use case beyond recreational boats."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A8", "A14"],
      "reason": "T1 (A6) names who changes behaviour (boat owners), why (perceived protection, brand trust) and how value is captured (direct sales, full margin). The chain contradicts itself, though. It rests on 'premium pricing' and 'willing to pay more', while the only source it cites (A8) says Jotun is 'cheaper than the competition'. A14 repeats 'premium' alongside 'competitive pricing'. Because of this inconsistency the chain amounts to a named benefit rather than a coherent path to captured value."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "T1 and T2 share the same customer (individual boat owners), the same product (Jotun marine coatings) and the same value lever (premium pricing). They differ only in the stated motive, protection/trust versus status. In effect they are rewordings of one adoption theory. No rival considers different customers, products or channels, or that consumer demand may be absent, even though A1 framed alternatives 2 and 3."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "The assumptions are stated, but the predictions are weak. 'DTC will generate higher average order value than existing B2B channels' (A6) is implausible as a test, because individual orders are naturally smaller than shipyard orders. T2's 'premium-priced coatings will generate higher average transaction value than standard offerings' (A7) is close to a tautology. Neither theory would be in real trouble if most of its predictions failed."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A8", "A9", "A13", "A14"],
      "reason": "A8 and A9 do give 'does not show' limits. However, A9 relabels a verified_observation as an 'independent_study' and counts a bare statement that Jotun supplies yacht coatings as support for a status-seeking motive. A13 and A14 treat a company marketing claim ('cheaper than the competition') as established fact ('The evidence shows ... well-priced relative to competitors'). They also treat product availability as a foundation for demand. No source is assessed for date, geography or fit with the target customer. The jotun-q1-2024-soft source is never formally assessed."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A10", "A11"],
      "reason": "A10 runs a search against T1 and retrieves jotun-q1-2024-soft, but the investigation never reports what that source implies for T1. A11 repeats essentially the same search. No search is made against T2 or against the case for investing at all. The search was made, but its result was not engaged with."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "A messaging A/B test could in principle separate the protection and status motives. The design has several flaws, however. The sample is internally inconsistent: 5,000 visitors, but 2,000 per track. The 10-percentage-point conversion threshold is an unjustified assumption and is unrealistic for ad conversion. The 'neither significant → do not invest' rule treats a tie between two messages as evidence of no demand. A recruited panel of boat owners cannot show market-level demand. The test cannot separate differentiated investment from alternatives 2 and 3. Cost and sample are proposed assumptions, not observations."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": [],
      "reason": "Revision is not in packet.applicable_dimensions, and no update was scheduled or delivered."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14", "A12"],
      "reason": "A14 commits to 'Invest in a differentiated consumer proposition' and in the same breath names, as the next action, a test whose decision rule includes 'do not invest'. The position is therefore not supported by the evidence and is internally inconsistent. It also rests on availability and company pricing claims rather than on any demand evidence. 'Would change if' conditions are given, but they are loose ('low consumer price sensitivity' would arguably support premium pricing rather than undermine it)."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14"],
      "reason": "Concludes that Jotun should invest on the basis that products are available on its website and a company claim of being cheaper than competitors. Neither shows consumer demand or willingness to pay. A marketing claim is treated as evidence of demand."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A14", "A8"],
      "reason": "The premium-pricing value capture contradicts the only cited source, which says Jotun prices below competitors. The recommendation asserts 'premium' and 'competitive pricing' at the same time."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "A verified_observation is mislabelled as an independent_study and used as support for a status-seeking consumer motive that it does not address."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "The test design is internally inconsistent (5,000 visitors versus 2,000 per track). The 10-percentage-point threshold is arbitrary, and the rule treats a messaging tie as a no-invest signal, so the test does not discriminate on the investment question."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A10", "A11"],
      "reason": "The challenge search retrieved contrary context (soft Scandinavian consumer demand) but never assessed or reported it."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is complete but has critical reasoning errors. The recommendation to invest rests on availability and a company pricing claim rather than demand evidence. The premium-pricing mechanism contradicts the cited source. A source is misclassified and stretched to support T2. The theories are near-rewordings, and the test's decision rule is flawed and internally inconsistent."
}

{
  "packet_id": "318776639dc8fe37",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: clinic/health-club deepening, no expansion and licensing to third-party consumer brands. It also adds the useful constraint of distinguishing current reach from potential demand. It does not identify a constraint a good analyst would not want missed, such as the measurement-variability problem for home self-tracking in tanita-pat-variation. It also corrects no premise."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A9), names who changes behaviour (home users already owning bathroom scales) and why (health-tool positioning drives regular use). Value capture is generic ('premium', 'retail channels'). It is not traced to why this manufacturer would capture the value, for example through its scale share or distribution. T2's value capture (A10) relies on an unsupported 'existing fitness equipment brand'."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 and T2 target different customers and channels: home weight management through retail, and athletes through sports retailers. However, they are templated segment variants with identical structure, not rival explanations of whether or why consumer demand exists. None of the framed alternatives from A1, such as clinic expansion or licensing, is developed as a theory."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable: survey intent, higher intent among weight-concerned users or exercisers, and regional sales concentration. They are weakly diagnostic, since stated survey intent could appear without real demand. 'Regions where health awareness is high' is vague. The assumptions are generic ('marketing can effectively communicate'). Neither theory would clearly be in serious trouble if the predictions failed."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15", "A16", "A2", "A5"],
      "reason": "Sources are mislabelled 'independent_study': in A11 a verified observation, in A16 a patent abstract. A16 treats a patent's device description as support for consumer demand. A12 and A15 treat the Jebb validation study as support for both segment theories, although it says nothing about buyers. They also do not note its wide individual limits (2SD 7.9 kg), which matter for personal tracking. A11 relies on an undated 'nowadays' statement about body-composition tracking with no caveat that it may postdate 1993. tanita-pat-variation was retrieved in A2 and A5 but never assessed, even though it directly bears on home repeated-measurement accuracy. The 'does not show' limits are boilerplate and omit date, place and customer-group fit."
    },
